      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand your concern about infection and the stress of delaying your project. While there are no home remedies that rival professional medical treatment for guaranteed infection prevention, you can try to minimize the risk as best as you can. First, wash the wound thoroughly with mild soap and warm water to remove any surface contaminants. Apply an antimicrobial ointment, such as those containing Neosporin, which contain bacitracin, polymyxin B, and neomycin to help combat bacteria. Dress the wound with a sterile, non-adhesive bandage, and change it daily, re-dressing it lightly each time to keep it clean. Adding zinc oxide paste inside the wound can also help create a barrier against bacteria. To speed up healing, some people use honey, an alternative with natural antibacterial properties, but not sure we recommend it for home treatment due to varying purity and direct wound contact complications. Keep an eye on it closely for any signs of infection, and please remember that seeking help from medical professionals when possible is the best course of action. The longer bacteria have to proliferate in a wound, the higher the risk of infection, and professional medical treatment is the safest solution in such situations.",
